window.LMS_LOAD && window.LMS_LOAD("multimodal", {"topics":[{"key":"multimodal/vision-language-models","sources":["Dosovitskiy et al., 2020 — An Image is Worth 16x16 Words (ViT)","Alayrac et al., 2022 — Flamingo: a Visual Language Model for Few-Shot Learning","Li et al., 2023 — BLIP-2: Bootstrapping Language-Image Pre-training with Frozen Image Encoders and Large Language Models","Liu et al., 2023 — Visual Instruction Tuning (LLaVA)"],"why":"Before VLMs, the way to get an LLM to \"see\" was a pipeline: run OCR or an image captioner, paste the\ntext into the prompt, and hope. That fails for a structural reason. A caption is a lossy compression\nchosen *before* the question is known. \"A bar chart of quarterly revenue\" cannot answer \"which quarter\ndropped, and by how much?\"; an OCR dump of an invoice loses which number sits in the *Total* box. Every\nstage in the pipeline also has its own error rate, and errors compound: the LLM reasons confidently\nover a mis-read digit it never had the chance to doubt.\n\nThe second problem is that text-only models have no grounding for spatial and visual concepts. They\nknow the word \"left of\" but have never been shown anything to the left of anything. Tasks like reading\na UI screenshot to click a button, checking whether a product photo matches its listing, or\ninterpreting a radiology scan need the model to reason over pixels and words in the same forward pass.\n\nThe naive fix, \"train a transformer on raw pixels and text from scratch\", is possible (early-fusion\nmodels do it) but expensive: the LLM has already learned language from trillions of tokens, and the\nvision encoder has already learned visual features from hundreds of millions of images. The dominant\nVLM design exists to *reuse* both and spend training only on the glue between them.","how":"### Step 1: the image becomes a sequence\nTransformers consume sequences, so the image must be serialised. A Vision Transformer (ViT) cuts the\nimage into fixed-size patches, flattens each patch's pixels, projects it with a linear layer, adds a\nposition embedding, and runs standard transformer blocks over the patch sequence. With 16×16-pixel\npatches a 224×224 image becomes 14 × 14 = 196 patch tokens; with 14-pixel patches at 336×336 (the\nCLIP ViT-L/14-336 encoder used by LLaVA-1.5) it becomes 24 × 24 = 576 tokens. The encoder is usually a\ncontrastively pretrained one (CLIP or SigLIP) *because* those encoders were trained to align visual\nfeatures with language already, which makes the later alignment step much cheaper than starting from a\nclassifier backbone.\n\n### Step 2: a connector maps vision features into the LLM's token space\nThe encoder's output vectors live in its own space, with its own dimension and statistics. A connector\ntranslates them into vectors the LLM can treat as if they were token embeddings. Three designs recur:\n\n- **Linear or MLP projector** (LLaVA): one vector per patch, projected to the LLM hidden size. Simple,\n  keeps all spatial detail, but every patch becomes an LLM token, so cost scales with resolution.\n- **Resampler / Q-Former** (Flamingo's Perceiver resampler, BLIP-2's Q-Former): a fixed set of learned\n  query vectors cross-attend to the patch features and emit a fixed number of tokens (e.g. 32 or 64)\n  regardless of image size. Cheaper per image, at the cost of compressing fine detail.\n- **Cross-attention into the LLM** (Flamingo, some Llama-vision variants): image features are not\n  inserted as tokens at all; gated cross-attention layers added between LLM blocks read them. The text\n  context is untouched, and the gates start at zero so the pretrained LLM is not disturbed at init.\n\n### Step 3: the LLM reasons over both\nAfter the connector, the input is a single sequence: text tokens, then image tokens at the point where\nthe image appears, then more text. Self-attention does the rest: a text query token can attend to the\npatch tokens that contain the relevant region. Nothing about the decoder changes; it still predicts the\nnext text token. This is why a VLM inherits its parent LLM's instruction following, tool calling and\nweaknesses almost unchanged.\n\n### Training recipe\nMost open VLMs follow a two-stage recipe. Stage one freezes both the vision encoder and the LLM and\ntrains only the connector on image-caption pairs, so the projector learns to speak the LLM's language\nwithout the LLM drifting. Stage two unfreezes the LLM (sometimes the encoder) and trains on visual\ninstruction data: questions about images, OCR-style tasks, charts, documents, grounding with bounding\nboxes. The instruction data is what turns \"can describe an image\" into \"can answer a question about it\".\n\n### Resolution, tiling and token cost\nFine detail (small text, chart labels) needs pixels, but a fixed-resolution encoder downsamples\neverything to one size. Common answers: tile a high-resolution image into several crops and feed each\ntile's tokens plus a downscaled global view (LLaVA-NeXT's \"AnyRes\"), or let the encoder accept variable\nnumbers of patches so token count follows native resolution (Qwen2-VL-style dynamic resolution). Either\nway, image tokens consume context and are billed as tokens; a single high-resolution page can cost on the\norder of a thousand tokens or more, an illustrative figure that varies by model and vendor. Pre-resizing\nimages to the detail you actually need is the cheapest optimisation available.\n\nThe arithmetic is worth doing by hand, because token count is quadratic in image side length:\n\n```\ntokens ≈ (H / (patch × merge)) × (W / (patch × merge))      # per tile, plus any global view\n```\n\nWith 14-pixel patches and a 2×2 token merge after the encoder (Qwen2-VL merges adjacent patches this\nway to cut sequence length 4×), a 1024 × 1024 image gives about (1024/28)² ≈ 1,340 tokens; halving the\nside to 512 gives about 335. Halving resolution quarters the cost, which is why a deliberate resize beats\nany prompt optimisation. A resampler breaks this relationship on purpose: its output count is fixed, so\ncost stops growing with resolution but so does the detail it can carry.\n\n### Outputs that point back into the image\nThe decoder only emits text, so \"where\" answers are serialised as text too: grounding-trained models\nemit bounding boxes as coordinate tokens (often normalised to a fixed grid such as 0–1000, convention\nvaries by model). This is what lets a computer-use agent turn \"click Submit\" into pixel coordinates, and\nit is why such coordinates must be rescaled to the original image if you resized it before sending.\n\n### Early-fusion variants\nSome models skip the separate encoder: Fuyu feeds linearly projected raw patches straight into the\ndecoder, and models described as \"natively multimodal\" are pretrained on interleaved image and text\nfrom the start. They avoid the encoder's resolution bottleneck and modality seam, at the price of far\nmore pretraining compute. For an application engineer the interface is identical.","example":"Any VLM served behind an OpenAI-compatible chat API (vLLM does this for open models; hosted vendors use\nthe same shape) accepts an image as a base64 data URL inside the message content. The snippet downsizes\nthe image first, asks a question that a caption could not answer, and requests a JSON-only reply for\nparsing (`response_format` is honoured by vLLM and most hosted APIs; if a server ignores it, strip code\nfences before `json.loads`).\n\n```python\nimport base64, io, json\nfrom PIL import Image\nfrom openai import OpenAI\n\nclient = OpenAI(base_url=\"http://localhost:8000/v1\", api_key=\"unused\")  # e.g. a vLLM server\nMODEL = \"<served-vlm-name>\"\n\ndef to_data_url(path, max_side=1024):\n    img = Image.open(path).convert(\"RGB\")\n    img.thumbnail((max_side, max_side))            # fewer pixels -> fewer image tokens\n    buf = io.BytesIO(); img.save(buf, format=\"JPEG\", quality=90)\n    return \"data:image/jpeg;base64,\" + base64.b64encode(buf.getvalue()).decode()\n\nquestion = (\"This is a revenue bar chart. Which quarter had the largest drop versus the \"\n            \"previous quarter, and what were the two values? Reply as JSON with keys \"\n            \"quarter, previous_value, value, units.\")\nresp = client.chat.completions.create(\n    model=MODEL,\n    messages=[{\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": [\n        {\"type\": \"text\", \"text\": question},\n        {\"type\": \"image_url\", \"image_url\": {\"url\": to_data_url(\"q_revenue.png\")}},\n    ]}],\n    temperature=0,\n    response_format={\"type\": \"json_object\"},\n)\nanswer = json.loads(resp.choices[0].message.content)\nprint(answer)   # {'quarter': 'Q3', 'previous_value': 4.2, 'value': 3.1, 'units': 'USD millions'}\nprint(resp.usage.prompt_tokens)  # watch this grow with image resolution\n```","tradeoffs":["Reusing a pretrained encoder and LLM makes training cheap and inherits strong language skills, but the encoder's fixed resolution caps fine detail; it flips when your inputs are dense documents or tiny text.","Projector designs keep every patch and preserve detail, while resamplers emit a fixed token budget and cut cost; choose the resampler when you process many images per request and detail matters less.","Tiling high-resolution images recovers small text at the cost of several times the tokens and latency, which is worth it for OCR-like tasks and wasteful for \"what is in this photo\" tasks.","A general VLM replaces a chain of specialised models (captioner, OCR, classifier) with one call, but gives no per-field confidence, so specialised models win when you need calibrated scores or audits."],"pitfalls":["Sending full-resolution screenshots by default; the symptom is prompt-token bills and latency that dwarf the text part of the request, with no accuracy gain over a resized image.","Trusting read-out numbers and strings without a check; VLMs hallucinate plausible digits in tables, and the symptom is answers that look right until reconciled against a total or a source system.","Asking for counts or exact spatial relations; many VLMs still count and localise poorly, and the symptom is confident wrong answers on \"how many\" and \"which is left of which\" that a human spots instantly.","Assuming image input is immune to prompt injection; text rendered inside an image is read as text, and the symptom is an agent following instructions embedded in a screenshot or a scanned document.","Evaluating on captioning quality when the task is question answering; the symptom is a model that describes images beautifully and fails the actual extraction your product needs.","Resizing or padding an image and then using returned box coordinates as if they referred to the original; the symptom is clicks or crops that land consistently offset or scaled from the target."],"when":"Reach for a VLM when the answer depends on information that a caption or OCR dump would discard: charts,\nUI screenshots for computer-use agents, product photos checked against text, forms with layout, visual\ninspection with a natural-language rubric. Prefer plain OCR when you only need the text and want per-word\nconfidence, and prefer CLIP-style embeddings when the job is search or classification over many images\nat low cost. Measure three things: task accuracy on a held-out set of your own images, image tokens per\nrequest at the resolution you ship, and hallucination rate on fields you can verify. In interviews,\nexpect to explain the encoder-connector-LLM pipeline, why the connector is trained first, and how\nresolution turns into tokens and cost.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Resizes and tiles images deliberately, pins image token counts in tests, and wraps VLM outputs in structured parsing plus validation before anything downstream trusts them.","architect":"Decides where a VLM replaces an OCR-plus-rules pipeline and where it merely supplements it, and sets the cost and latency budget per page or screenshot.","fde":"Benchmarks the candidate VLM on the customer's real scans and screenshots, finds the resolution at which accuracy plateaus, and documents the failure modes the customer's reviewers must catch."},"check":{"q":"A customer wants to extract line items from scanned invoices with a VLM. Accuracy is good on\nclean samples but small-font quantities are frequently wrong. What is happening and what would you\nchange, in order of cost?","a":"The vision encoder runs at a fixed input resolution, so a full page is downsampled until small\ndigits occupy a patch or less and become unreadable; the LLM then fills in plausible values. Cheapest\nfix: crop the line-item region or tile the page so each tile is near the encoder's native resolution,\nsending a downscaled global view alongside. Next: choose a model with dynamic resolution or a larger\ntile budget and measure the token cost increase. Then: run a classic OCR pass and give the VLM the text\nwith coordinates as extra context so it reads rather than guesses. Finally, add validation such as\nquantity × unit price ≈ line total so remaining errors are caught instead of silently accepted."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why do most open VLMs train the connector first with the LLM frozen?","options":["The connector is the largest component and needs the most data","It aligns visual features to the LLM's input space without letting the LLM's language ability drift","Freezing the LLM is required because image tokens cannot carry gradients","It allows the vision encoder to be trained from scratch on captions"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The LLM already knows language; stage one only teaches the small projector to emit vectors the LLM understands. Unfreezing everything on caption data would degrade the LLM's instruction following."},{"q":"What primarily determines how many context tokens an image consumes in a projector-style VLM?","options":["The file size in bytes after JPEG compression","The number of distinct objects detected in the image","The number of encoder patches, which follows image resolution and tiling","The length of the user's text question"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Each patch becomes one token after projection, so a 24 × 24 patch grid yields 576 tokens and tiling multiplies that. Compression and object count do not change the patch grid."},{"q":"A pipeline captions images with one model and sends the caption to an LLM. What is the core limitation a VLM removes?","options":["Captioners cannot run on GPUs efficiently","LLMs cannot read captions longer than a sentence","The caption discards details before the question is known, so later questions cannot recover them","Captions are always in a different language from the prompt"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A caption is a lossy summary fixed in advance; a VLM attends to the pixels in the same forward pass as the question, so it can look up details the question demands rather than relying on what was kept."}]},{"key":"multimodal/clip","sources":["Radford et al., 2021 — Learning Transferable Visual Models From Natural Language Supervision","van den Oord et al., 2018 — Representation Learning with Contrastive Predictive Coding","Zhai et al., 2023 — Sigmoid Loss for Language Image Pre-Training (SigLIP)","Cherti et al., 2023 — Reproducible Scaling Laws for Contrastive Language-Image Learning (OpenCLIP)"],"why":"Supervised image classifiers learn a closed vocabulary. An ImageNet model knows 1,000 nouns and nothing\nelse; to add \"forklift blocking a fire exit\" you collect and label thousands of images, retrain, and\nstill cannot ask about \"a forklift blocking a *different* exit\". The labelling cost is the limiting\nfactor, and the learned features are biased toward whatever distinguishes the chosen classes.\n\nThe web already contains hundreds of millions of images with text next to them: alt text, captions,\nproduct titles. That text is noisy but free and open-vocabulary. The question is how to learn from it.\nThe obvious route, train a captioning model that predicts the exact words, spends enormous capacity on\npredicting the *phrasing* of a caption (\"a photo of\", \"stock image\") rather than its content, and the\nCLIP authors found it learned far more slowly than a bag-of-words baseline.\n\nThe reframing that works is to stop predicting words and instead predict *which caption goes with which\nimage*. That is a matching problem: among a batch of pairs, pick the right partner. It only needs the\nrepresentation to capture content that distinguishes captions from each other, which is exactly the\nsemantic content a downstream search or classifier wants. CLIP exists because matching scales where\ngeneration and labelling did not.","how":"### Two encoders, one space\nAn image encoder (ResNet or, in the stronger variants, a Vision Transformer such as ViT-B/32 or\nViT-L/14) maps an image to a vector. A text encoder (a transformer over byte-pair-encoded text with a\nmaximum length of 77 tokens) maps a caption to a vector. Each is followed by a linear projection to a\ncommon dimension (512 for ViT-B/32, 768 for ViT-L/14) and L2-normalised, so similarity is a cosine\nsimilarity, a dot product of unit vectors.\n\n### The contrastive objective\nTake a batch of N (image, text) pairs. Compute all N × N cosine similarities between every image and\nevery text, scaled by a learned temperature (initialised so that logits are multiplied by about 1/0.07\nand clipped so the scale never exceeds 100). The N diagonal entries are the true pairs; the N² − N\noff-diagonal entries are negatives. The loss is a symmetric cross-entropy: for each image, a softmax\nover the N texts should put its mass on the matching text, and for each text, a softmax over the N\nimages should pick the matching image. The two directions are averaged. This is the InfoNCE loss; it\npushes matching pairs together and all other pairs in the batch apart. Written out, with unit vectors\n`x_i` (images), `y_j` (texts) and learned scale `s = 1/T`:\n\n```\nlogits[i, j] = s · (x_i · y_j)                         # N × N matrix\nL_img→txt    = −(1/N) Σ_i log( exp(logits[i,i]) / Σ_j exp(logits[i,j]) )\nL_txt→img    = −(1/N) Σ_j log( exp(logits[j,j]) / Σ_i exp(logits[i,j]) )\nL            = (L_img→txt + L_txt→img) / 2\n```\n\nIn code this is two `cross_entropy(logits, arange(N))` calls, one on the matrix and one on its\ntranspose. The temperature is learned rather than fixed because the right sharpness changes during\ntraining: too soft and the gradient from hard negatives vanishes, too sharp and training is unstable.\n\nTwo consequences follow. First, the batch *is* the negative set, so larger batches give harder, more\ninformative negatives; CLIP used a batch of 32,768 pairs, and the training data was about 400 million\npairs. Second, the loss is relative: it never says what absolute similarity a match should have, only\nthat the match must beat the others in the batch. That is why raw CLIP scores are not calibrated\nprobabilities and why thresholds must be tuned per task.\n\n### Zero-shot classification by prompting\nTo classify an image into classes you never trained on, write each class as a sentence, \"a photo of a\n{label}\", encode all sentences, encode the image, and take the softmax over the cosine similarities. The\ntext encoder turns a class list into a classifier weight matrix on the fly. Prompt ensembling (averaging\n\"a photo of a {}\", \"a blurry photo of a {}\", \"a drawing of a {}\") improves accuracy because the training\ncaptions were varied too. Reported zero-shot ImageNet top-1 for the largest CLIP variant was in the\nmid-70s percent, matching a supervised ResNet-50 without using any ImageNet labels; the exact number is\nin the paper.\n\n### Why this representation transfers\nBecause training only rewarded distinguishing captions, the encoder learns whatever visual evidence\ncorrelates with words: objects, scenes, styles, text in the image, brands. It does not learn to count\nor to encode precise spatial layout, because captions rarely make those distinctions. The representation\nis therefore excellent for retrieval and coarse classification and weak for compositional reasoning,\nwhich is the reason VLMs bolt an LLM on top of it rather than using it alone.\n\n### The modality gap\n\"One shared space\" is looser than it sounds. In practice image embeddings and text embeddings occupy\ntwo separated regions of the sphere: a caption is closer to its own image than to other images, but\nalmost any image is closer to other images than to any text. The loss only requires the right ordering\nwithin each row, not that the two clouds overlap. Practically, cross-modal cosines sit in a narrow low\nband while image-to-image cosines sit much higher, so a threshold tuned for one direction is meaningless\nin the other, and mixing image and text vectors in one nearest-neighbour index without care ranks all\nsame-modality items first.\n\n### Zero-shot versus linear probe\nZero-shot uses the text encoder to build the classifier. When you have even a few labelled examples per\nclass, a logistic-regression \"linear probe\" trained on frozen image embeddings usually beats the\nzero-shot classifier, because it learns which embedding directions matter for *your* classes instead of\nrelying on how well a sentence describes them. The CLIP paper notes that with very few examples per\nclass a probe can underperform zero-shot; once labels exist in modest numbers, the probe is the cheap\nupgrade to try before any fine-tuning.\n\n### Successors and variants\nOpenCLIP reproduced and scaled the recipe on public data (LAION). SigLIP replaces the batch-wide softmax\nwith an independent sigmoid loss per pair, so each (image, text) pair is a binary \"match or not\"\ndecision: `−log σ(z_ij · (t · x_i·y_j + b))` with `z_ij = +1` for the true pair and `−1` otherwise,\nand a learned bias `b`, initialised strongly negative, that offsets the fact that almost every pair is a\nnegative. Because no row has to be normalised across the whole batch, the batch can be split across\ndevices without gathering all embeddings for one softmax, memory scales better, and it trains well at\nsmaller batch sizes; many recent VLMs use SigLIP encoders as their vision tower. The mechanism, two encoders aligned by\npositives-versus-negatives, is unchanged.","example":"Zero-shot classification with the Hugging Face CLIP implementation, then the same thing by hand with\nthe raw embeddings so the cosine-similarity-and-softmax mechanism is visible. The second form is what\nyou reuse for search: encode once, compare many times.\n\n```python\nimport torch\nfrom PIL import Image\nfrom transformers import CLIPModel, CLIPProcessor\n\nname = \"openai/clip-vit-base-patch32\"\nmodel = CLIPModel.from_pretrained(name).eval()\nproc = CLIPProcessor.from_pretrained(name)\n\nimage = Image.open(\"warehouse.jpg\").convert(\"RGB\")\nlabels = [\"a forklift\", \"a pallet of boxes\", \"an empty aisle\", \"a person in a hi-vis vest\"]\nprompts = [f\"a photo of {l}\" for l in labels]\n\nwith torch.no_grad():\n    out = model(**proc(text=prompts, images=image, return_tensors=\"pt\", padding=True))\n    probs = out.logits_per_image.softmax(dim=-1)[0]        # scaled cosine sims -> softmax\nfor l, p in zip(labels, probs):\n    print(f\"{p:.3f}  {l}\")\n\n# Same mechanism by hand: unit vectors, dot product, temperature.\nwith torch.no_grad():\n    img = model.get_image_features(**proc(images=image, return_tensors=\"pt\"))\n    txt = model.get_text_features(**proc(text=prompts, return_tensors=\"pt\", padding=True))\nimg = img / img.norm(dim=-1, keepdim=True)                # [1, 512]\ntxt = txt / txt.norm(dim=-1, keepdim=True)                # [4, 512]\ncos = img @ txt.T                                          # cosine similarities, typically 0.2-0.35\nprint(cos, (cos * model.logit_scale.exp()).softmax(-1))   # logit_scale.exp() is the learned 1/T\n```\n\nThe hand-computed cosines are small and close together (the shared space is not spread out), which is\nwhy the learned scale of roughly 100 is needed before the softmax produces a decisive distribution.","tradeoffs":["Open-vocabulary classification with no labelling costs, but accuracy on fine-grained or domain-specific classes (medical, industrial parts) lags a supervised model trained on even a few thousand labels.","Contrastive training is sample-efficient compared with caption generation, but needs very large batches and web-scale data, so training your own CLIP is rarely worth it versus fine-tuning OpenCLIP.","A single shared space enables text-to-image and image-to-image search from one index, yet scores are uncalibrated, so each use direction needs its own threshold tuned on labelled examples.","Captions as supervision teach what is in an image, not how many or where; when the task is counting, reading layout or spatial relations, a VLM or a detector is the right tool instead."],"pitfalls":["Treating softmax outputs as probabilities of correctness; the symptom is high-confidence predictions for images that match none of the offered labels, because softmax must sum to one over your list.","Using bare labels instead of sentences; the symptom is a measurable accuracy drop, since training text looked like captions, and \"a photo of a dog\" matches the distribution far better than \"dog\".","Expecting robustness to text in images; the symptom is an object with a handwritten label being classified as the label's word (the typographic-attack effect), which matters for moderation and retail.","Comparing scores across encoders or across text-to-image versus image-to-image queries; the symptom is a threshold that works in one direction and silently returns garbage in the other.","Truncating long descriptions silently at 77 tokens; the symptom is that a detailed query's later constraints have no effect on the results."],"when":"Use CLIP-family encoders when you need to search, filter, cluster or coarsely classify images by\ndescription without training data: content moderation prefilters, product search, deduplication,\nlabelling candidate images for a dataset, or as the vision tower when assembling a VLM. Avoid it as the\nfinal decision maker where errors are costly and fine-grained; there, use it to narrow candidates and\nlet a supervised model or a VLM decide. Measure recall@k on a labelled retrieval set and accuracy at a\ntuned threshold, not raw similarity values. Interviewers ask you to write the contrastive loss on a\nwhiteboard, explain why batch size matters, and say why zero-shot works at all; those three answers\ncover the idea.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Encodes images and text with the same checkpoint, stores normalised vectors, and tunes per-direction thresholds on a labelled sample before exposing search or filtering.","architect":"Chooses between a CLIP-style retrieval layer and a VLM per task, and fixes the encoder version as an index dependency so re-embedding is planned, not accidental.","fde":"Builds a quick zero-shot classifier from a customer's taxonomy written as sentences, shows the accuracy ceiling on their images, and uses the gaps to justify labelling or fine-tuning."},"check":{"q":"You index a retailer's catalogue with CLIP so shoppers can type queries. Text-to-image search\nworks, but the \"similar items\" feature built on image-to-image similarity returns near-identical\ncolours and ignores category. Why, and what would you do?","a":"The contrastive loss shapes the space around what captions distinguish, and image-to-image\ndistances are dominated by global appearance (colour, background, style) rather than by category,\nbecause no caption ever told the model that two differently coloured jackets are the same product type.\nText and image embeddings also occupy different regions, so a threshold tuned on text queries does not\ntransfer. Options: compute similarity in the text space by first classifying each image into the catalogue\ntaxonomy with zero-shot prompts and restricting neighbours to the same class; fine-tune the encoder with\nproduct pairs as positives; or combine CLIP similarity with structured attributes in a hybrid ranker and\nevaluate recall@k on labelled \"similar item\" judgements."},"quiz":[{"q":"In CLIP's contrastive loss, where do the negative examples come from?","options":["A separately curated set of mismatched captions written by annotators","The other pairs in the same training batch","Random noise vectors added to the image embeddings","Images with the same label from a classification dataset"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Every image is contrasted against all other captions in the batch and vice versa, so a batch of N pairs supplies N² − N negatives for free; this is why very large batches help."},{"q":"Why can CLIP classify into categories it never saw during training?","options":["Its image encoder was pretrained on ImageNet with all possible labels","The text encoder stores a lookup table of class names","Class names written as captions are encoded into the shared space and act as classifier weights","It retrieves similar images from the training set at inference time"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Zero-shot classification is retrieval over text: encode \"a photo of a {label}\" for each class and pick the closest to the image embedding. No training-set images are stored or needed."},{"q":"Which task is CLIP's representation least suited to on its own?","options":["Finding duplicate product photos across a catalogue","Filtering out images that do not contain a logo","Reporting how many people stand to the left of a doorway","Grouping stock photos by scene type"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Captions rarely encode counts or precise spatial relations, so the contrastive objective never rewarded learning them. Retrieval, filtering and scene grouping are exactly what caption matching teaches."}]},{"key":"multimodal/image-embeddings","sources":["Radford et al., 2021 — Learning Transferable Visual Models From Natural Language Supervision","Oquab et al., 2023 — DINOv2: Learning Robust Visual Features without Supervision","Liang et al., 2022 — Mind the Gap: Understanding the Modality Gap in Multi-modal Contrastive Representation Learning","Faysse et al., 2024 — ColPali: Efficient Document Retrieval with Vision Language Models"],"why":"Ask a database \"find images like this one\" and it has nothing to work with. Raw pixel distance says a\nphoto and its slightly cropped copy are far apart while a dark photo of a cat and a dark photo of a car\nare close. Perceptual hashes (pHash and friends) fix the first problem, near-duplicate detection, by\nhashing coarse frequency structure, but they still know nothing about content: a different photo of the\nsame product hashes to something unrelated. Tags and metadata solve content search only if someone\nwrote the tags, which does not scale and does not cover what the tagger did not think of.\n\nWhat is needed is a function that maps an image to a point in a space where distance tracks meaning.\nDeep networks produce such a function as a by-product of training on a task: the activations just\nbefore the final classifier of an ImageNet model already group dogs with dogs. The modern versions are\ntrained *for* this property directly, by contrastive or self-distillation objectives, and some are\ntrained jointly with text so that a sentence and an image can be compared in the same space. Once\nimages are vectors, every tool built for text embeddings, approximate nearest-neighbour indexes,\nclustering, outlier detection, works unchanged.","how":"### The encoder decides the geometry\nThree families of encoder give three different notions of similarity, and choosing wrong is the most\ncommon failure:\n\n- **Supervised classifier backbones** (ResNet or ViT trained on ImageNet, penultimate layer): cheap and\n  fast, but the space is organised around the training label set. Images that share a label cluster;\n  distinctions the labels never made (which brand, which colour variant) are weak.\n- **Self-supervised visual encoders** (DINO, DINOv2): trained to make different augmentations of the\n  same image agree, with no text and no labels. A student network sees small crops, a teacher (a moving\n  average of the student's weights) sees larger crops of the same image, and the student is trained to\n  match the teacher's output, so the features must encode what survives cropping, colour jitter and\n  scale changes: object identity and shape rather than pixels. They produce strong *visual* similarity, including dense\n  per-patch features useful for localisation, but they cannot be queried with text.\n- **Contrastive image-text encoders** (CLIP, SigLIP, OpenCLIP): trained to match images to captions.\n  Their image vectors live in a space shared with text vectors, so one index serves both \"find images\n  like this\" and \"find images of a red forklift\". Similarity is semantic, caption-like, and weaker on\n  purely visual detail than DINOv2.\n\nCommercial multimodal embedding APIs are usually of the third kind. For scanned pages, a fourth option\nis a document-screenshot embedder such as ColPali, which keeps one vector per patch and scores queries by\nlate interaction (sum over query tokens of the best-matching patch), trading index size (on the order\nof a thousand small vectors per page instead of one) for far better retrieval of text-heavy page images\nthan a single pooled vector.\n\n### From pixels to one vector\nThe image is resized and normalised to the encoder's input size (often 224 or 336 pixels on a side;\nmany pipelines centre-crop, which silently discards the edges), split into patches, and run through\nthe network. The patch outputs are pooled into one vector, either by taking a dedicated CLS token or by\naveraging patches, then passed through a projection head. The vector is L2-normalised *because* the\ntraining loss compared directions, not magnitudes; after normalisation, cosine similarity equals the dot\nproduct and a vector index can use inner product directly. Dimensions in common models run from a few\nhundred to a bit over a thousand; higher is not better by itself, it is only what the model was trained\nto emit.\n\n### Similarity, thresholds and the modality gap\nNearest-neighbour search ranks candidates correctly without any threshold. Deciding whether a match is\n\"good enough\" (duplicate or not, on-topic or not) needs one, and thresholds are model-specific: CLIP\nimage-image cosines for near-duplicates may sit above 0.9 while a correct text-to-image match may score\naround 0.3, illustrative numbers that vary by checkpoint. The reason text and image scores live in\ndifferent ranges is the modality gap: in contrastive models, all image vectors occupy one cone of the\nspace and all text vectors another, so cross-modal similarities are systematically lower than\nwithin-modal ones. Tune separate thresholds per query direction on labelled examples.\n\n### When the off-the-shelf geometry is wrong\nIf your notion of \"similar\" is not what any pretrained objective rewarded (same SKU across colourways,\nsame defect type across parts), no threshold will fix it, because the neighbours themselves are wrong.\nThe usual escalation is: re-rank top-k candidates with metadata or a VLM; train a small linear or MLP\nadapter on frozen embeddings using labelled positive and negative pairs (cheap, and the base index can\nbe re-projected without re-encoding images); or fine-tune the encoder with a contrastive or triplet loss\non your pairs, which moves the space most but forces a full re-embed and risks forgetting general\nfeatures. Start from the cheapest step and measure recall@k on held-out pairs after each one.\n\n### Where embeddings go\nEach vector is stored with an identifier and metadata in a vector index; an approximate nearest-neighbour\nstructure (HNSW or IVF) makes search sublinear. The same practices as text embeddings apply: record the\nmodel name and version with every vector, because vectors from two checkpoints are not comparable and a\nmodel upgrade means a full re-index; keep the preprocessing (resize, crop, colour conversion) identical\nat index and query time, because it is part of the model.\n\nStorage is simple arithmetic: `n_images × dim × bytes_per_value`. Ten million 768-d float32 vectors are\n10⁷ × 768 × 4 ≈ 31 GB before index overhead; float16 halves that, int8 scalar quantisation quarters it,\nand binary or product quantisation shrink it further at a recall cost you measure, typically recovered by\nre-scoring the top candidates with full-precision vectors.","example":"Embed a folder of images with a CLIP checkpoint via sentence-transformers, which accepts PIL images\ndirectly, then answer both an image query and a text query from the same matrix. Normalisation is done\nby the library so cosine similarity is a plain matrix product. Sizes are small enough for NumPy; swap\nin an ANN index once the matrix stops fitting in memory.\n\n```python\nimport numpy as np\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfrom PIL import Image\nfrom sentence_transformers import SentenceTransformer\n\nmodel = SentenceTransformer(\"clip-ViT-B-32\")               # image + text encoders, 512-d, normalised\npaths = sorted(Path(\"catalog\").glob(\"*.jpg\"))\nimages = [Image.open(p).convert(\"RGB\") for p in paths]\nE = model.encode(images, batch_size=64, normalize_embeddings=True)   # shape [n_images, 512]\nnp.save(\"catalog_clip-ViT-B-32.npy\", E)                    # name the file after the model version\n\ndef top_k(query_vec, k=5):\n    sims = E @ query_vec                                    # cosine similarity, since both are unit length\n    idx = np.argsort(-sims)[:k]\n    return [(paths[i].name, float(sims[i])) for i in idx]\n\nq_img = model.encode(Image.open(\"query.jpg\").convert(\"RGB\"), normalize_embeddings=True)\nprint(\"image->image\", top_k(q_img))     # near-duplicates score high, e.g. > 0.9 (illustrative)\n\nq_txt = model.encode(\"a red forklift in a warehouse\", normalize_embeddings=True)\nprint(\"text->image \", top_k(q_txt))     # correct matches score lower, e.g. ~0.3 (modality gap)\n\n# Duplicate sweep: pairwise sims above a tuned threshold within the catalogue itself.\nS = E @ E.T\nnp.fill_diagonal(S, 0)\ndupes = np.argwhere(np.triu(S) > 0.95)\nprint(len(dupes), \"candidate duplicate pairs\")\n# memory: E is 10,000 x 512 x 4 bytes = ~20 MB, but S is 10,000 x 10,000 x 4 bytes = 400 MB;\n# past ~10k images compute S in row blocks or ask an ANN index for each row's top-k instead.\n```","tradeoffs":["Contrastive image-text encoders give one index for text and image queries, but their purely visual similarity is weaker than DINOv2's; pick the self-supervised encoder when queries are always images.","One pooled vector per image is compact and fast to search, while per-patch (late-interaction) embeddings retrieve dense pages far better at roughly two orders of magnitude more storage per page (about a thousand 128-d vectors versus one) and a costlier scoring step.","Centre-cropping and resizing to a small square keep encoding cheap but drop edge content and fine print; when small details decide relevance, tile the image and index each tile.","A bigger model yields better neighbours yet multiplies indexing cost over millions of images; for dedup or coarse grouping a small checkpoint is usually sufficient and worth measuring first."],"pitfalls":["Mixing vectors from different checkpoints or preprocessing in one index; the symptom is retrieval quality that collapses after a \"minor\" model upgrade without any error being raised.","Reusing a threshold across query directions; the symptom is text queries returning nothing at a threshold tuned for image-to-image, or everything at one tuned the other way.","Using a semantic encoder for exact-duplicate detection; the symptom is \"duplicates\" that are merely similar products, or missed duplicates that differ by a watermark the model considers meaningful.","Skipping EXIF orientation and colour-space handling; the symptom is phone photos embedded upside down or in CMYK garbage, so matches fail for a subset of uploads only.","Forgetting that the index stores content; embeddings of sensitive images are still sensitive data and can be inverted or matched, and the symptom is a privacy review that blocks launch."],"when":"Reach for image embeddings when the job is search, deduplication, clustering, recommendation or\nnearest-neighbour moderation over many images, where each item is compared with many others and a\nper-image LLM call would be too slow or expensive. Use a VLM instead when you need a reasoned answer\nabout one image, and OCR or a document parser when the content is text. Decide the encoder by the query\ntype: text queries need a contrastive image-text model; image-only queries favour self-supervised\nencoders; page images favour late-interaction document embedders. Measure recall@k against labelled\njudgements and the precision at your chosen threshold. Interview questions typically probe why cosine\nsimilarity, why normalisation, and why text-to-image scores differ from image-to-image.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Owns the preprocessing-encode-normalise-store path, versions the embedding model in the index metadata, and keeps query-time preprocessing byte-identical to index-time preprocessing.","architect":"Picks the encoder family per query type, sizes the index (vectors × dimension × bytes), and plans re-indexing as a scheduled migration rather than an emergency.","fde":"Runs a labelled similarity study on the customer's own images to set thresholds, and shows them where embedding search ends and human or VLM review must begin."},"check":{"q":"A moderation team wants to block re-uploads of previously removed images, including recompressed,\nresized and lightly cropped copies, with near-zero false positives. Which embedding approach and matching\npolicy would you propose, and why not a general CLIP index alone?","a":"The task is near-duplicate detection, not semantic similarity. A CLIP space clusters by meaning,\nso two different photos of a similar scene can score higher than a cropped copy of the banned image,\ngiving both false positives and misses. Propose a two-stage policy: a cheap perceptual hash or a\nself-supervised visual encoder tuned for copy detection as the first filter, with a conservative\nthreshold set on a labelled set of true copies versus near-miss lookalikes, then a second check (pixel\nalignment or human review) before blocking. Store the model version with each vector, keep preprocessing\nidentical at ingest and query, and measure false-positive rate on legitimate uploads because that is the\nconstraint the team stated."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why are image embeddings L2-normalised before they are stored in a vector index?","options":["Normalisation compresses the vectors so they take less disk space","The encoders were trained on direction, so cosine similarity is the meaningful metric and it becomes a plain dot product","Vector databases cannot store values larger than one","It removes the modality gap between text and image vectors"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Contrastive and self-supervised losses compare angles, not magnitudes. Unit vectors make inner product equal cosine similarity, which ANN indexes compute fast; normalisation does not change storage or the gap."},{"q":"A team needs \"find photos matching this sentence\" and \"find photos like this photo\" from one index. Which encoder family fits?","options":["An ImageNet-supervised ResNet's penultimate layer","A self-supervised encoder such as DINOv2","A contrastive image-text encoder such as CLIP or SigLIP","A perceptual hash"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Only image-text contrastive models place text and images in one shared space, so both query types hit the same vectors. DINOv2 and ResNet features cannot be queried with text at all."},{"q":"After tuning a similarity threshold of 0.85 on image-to-image matches, text queries return no results. What is the most likely cause?","options":["The text encoder was not loaded correctly","Text embeddings have a different dimension from image embeddings","Cross-modal similarities are systematically lower than within-modal ones because of the modality gap","The vector index only supports image vectors"],"answer":2,"explanation":"In contrastive models, image and text vectors occupy separate regions, so a correct text-to-image match scores well below a correct image-to-image match. Thresholds must be tuned per query direction."}]},{"key":"multimodal/ocr","sources":["Smith, 2007 — An Overview of the Tesseract OCR Engine","Graves et al., 2006 — Connectionist Temporal Classification","Shi et al., 2017 — An End-to-End Trainable Neural Network for Image-based Sequence Recognition (CRNN)","Li et al., 2021 — TrOCR: Transformer-based Optical Character Recognition with Pre-trained Models"],"why":"A PDF generated from Word carries its text as text; a PDF produced by a scanner is a picture of a page.\nFaxes, phone photos of receipts, screenshots, ID cards and engineering drawings are all pictures. To a\ntext pipeline they are opaque blobs. The naive fix, \"ask a vision-language model to read it\", works for\na page or two but fails as a system: it is slow and expensive per page, it gives no per-word\nconfidence, it cannot return coordinates for highlighting or redaction, and when a digit is unclear it\nproduces a plausible digit instead of a flagged one. Invoices and medical records cannot absorb that.\n\nOCR exists as a dedicated capability because recognising printed characters is a well-posed problem\nwith decades of engineering behind it: deterministic output, word-level confidence, bounding boxes,\nthousands of pages per hour on a CPU. The real difficulty was never the letter \"A\" in a clean font. It\nis deciding which pixels are text, in what order they should be read, what to do with a skewed\nlow-resolution scan, and how a table's cells map to rows. Modern OCR is a pipeline that attacks each of\nthose separately, and the engineering judgement is about which stage is failing when the text comes out\nwrong.","how":"### Stage 1: preprocessing\nRecognition accuracy depends on input quality more than on the recogniser. Typical steps: convert to\ngreyscale, upscale small images (classic engines are tuned for roughly 300 dots per inch, a widely\nused rule of thumb), deskew by estimating the dominant text angle, binarise with an adaptive threshold\nso uneven lighting does not turn half the page black, and remove noise and borders. Each step exists\nbecause the recogniser was trained on clean, horizontal, high-contrast glyphs and degrades sharply\noutside that distribution.\n\n### Stage 2: detection and layout analysis\nThe engine must find text before reading it. Classic engines (Tesseract) use connected-component\nanalysis: group blobs of ink into characters, characters into lines, lines into blocks, and infer\nreading order from block geometry. Deep detectors (CRAFT, DBNet, used by EasyOCR and PaddleOCR) are\nsegmentation networks that predict a text-probability map and extract word or line polygons, which is\nfar more robust on photos, curved text and cluttered backgrounds. Layout analysis also assigns a page\nsegmentation mode: single column, multi-column, single line, sparse text. Choosing the wrong mode is\nthe most common reason columns get interleaved.\n\n### Stage 3: recognition\nEach detected line or word image is recognised as a sequence. The dominant design is a CNN or\ntransformer feature extractor over the image width, producing one feature vector per horizontal slice,\nfollowed by a sequence model and the CTC loss (Connectionist Temporal Classification). CTC lets the\nnetwork emit a character or a blank (written ∅ here) at every slice without knowing character\nboundaries in advance. Decoding applies two rules in order: collapse runs of the same symbol, then delete\nblanks.\n\n```\nslices:          h h e ∅ l l ∅ l o o\ncollapse runs:   h e ∅ l ∅ l o\ndrop blanks:     h e l l o          -> \"hello\"\nwithout the ∅ between the l's, \"h e l l l o\" collapses to \"helo\"\n```\n\nThe blank is therefore what lets the network express a genuine double letter. Training works because\nmany slice sequences decode to the same string; CTC's loss is the negative log of the *sum* of the\nprobabilities of all of them, computed efficiently with a forward-backward dynamic programme, so the\nnetwork is never told which slice holds which glyph. The design choice matters for failure analysis:\nbecause each slice is predicted largely independently, a CTC recogniser has no strong notion of which\ncharacter sequences are plausible, which is why a separate language model is bolted on afterwards.\nTesseract moved to an LSTM-plus-CTC recogniser in version 4. Transformer encoder-decoder recognisers (TrOCR) drop CTC and generate characters\nautoregressively, which handles handwriting and irregular text better at higher cost.\n\n### Stage 4: post-processing and output\nRaw recognitions are refined with a dictionary or a character-level language model (which fixes \"rn\"\nversus \"m\" in words but can *introduce* errors in codes and IDs, which is why engines let you disable\nit). The output is structured: for each word, its text, a confidence score, and a bounding box, often in\nhOCR or ALTO formats. Confidence is the handle for everything downstream: route low-confidence fields to\na human, re-run a region at higher resolution, or decline to extract.\n\n### Measuring it\nCharacter error rate (CER) is (substitutions + deletions + insertions) / characters in the reference;\nword error rate (WER) is the same over words. Measure on your own scans, because published numbers come\nfrom clean benchmarks. Separately track layout errors (wrong reading order, merged columns), which CER\non a flattened string hides.\n\n### The alternatives named in practice\nCloud document OCR (AWS Textract, Google Document AI, Azure Document Intelligence) bundles detection,\nrecognition, tables and form key-value pairs with strong accuracy, charged per page and subject to data\nresidency. VLM-based OCR reads anything a human can, including messy layouts, but is slower, costlier,\nand hallucinates under uncertainty with no confidence to warn you. Classic engines are free, run\noffline on CPUs and give boxes and confidences, but need preprocessing care and struggle with handwriting\nand photos. Most production systems combine them: a cheap engine first, escalation on low confidence.","example":"Tesseract through pytesseract, with the preprocessing steps that make the biggest difference and the\nword-level data structure that lets you act on confidence rather than trusting a flat string. Requires\nthe Tesseract binary and OpenCV.\n\n```python\nimport cv2, pytesseract\nfrom pytesseract import Output\n\ndef preprocess(path, min_width=2000):\n    img = cv2.imread(path)\n    if img.shape[1] < min_width:                      # upscale small scans toward ~300 dpi\n        s = min_width / img.shape[1]\n        img = cv2.resize(img, None, fx=s, fy=s, interpolation=cv2.INTER_CUBIC)\n    gray = cv2.cvtColor(img, cv2.COLOR_BGR2GRAY)\n    return cv2.adaptiveThreshold(gray, 255, cv2.ADAPTIVE_THRESH_GAUSSIAN_C,\n                                 cv2.THRESH_BINARY, 31, 15)   # handles uneven lighting\n\nimg = preprocess(\"invoice_scan.jpg\")\ncfg = \"--oem 1 --psm 6 -l eng\"       # LSTM engine; psm 6 = assume a uniform block of text\nd = pytesseract.image_to_data(img, config=cfg, output_type=Output.DICT)\n\nwords = []\nfor text, conf, x, y, w, h in zip(d[\"text\"], d[\"conf\"], d[\"left\"], d[\"top\"], d[\"width\"], d[\"height\"]):\n    if text.strip():                                   # conf is -1 for non-word boxes\n        words.append({\"text\": text, \"conf\": float(conf), \"box\": (x, y, w, h)})\n\nlow = [w for w in words if w[\"conf\"] < 60]             # threshold tuned on your scans\nprint(len(words), \"words;\", len(low), \"below confidence threshold\")\nfor w in low[:5]:\n    print(w)                                           # e.g. {'text': '1O2.5O', 'conf': 41.0, 'box': (...)}\n# Route pages with many low-confidence amount fields to a VLM pass or human review,\n# and keep the boxes: they let you highlight, redact, and crop regions for re-OCR.\n```","tradeoffs":["Classic engines are free, offline and give per-word confidence and boxes, but need preprocessing and degrade on photos and handwriting; cloud OCR flips the trade when layout and tables matter most.","A character language model raises accuracy on prose while corrupting serial numbers and codes, so disable dictionary correction for fields that are not words.","Higher resolution improves recognition up to a point, after which cost rises with no gain; find the plateau on your own documents rather than scanning everything at maximum DPI.","VLM OCR handles arbitrary layouts in one call but trades away confidence scores and determinism, which is acceptable for summarisation and unacceptable for amounts that feed a ledger."],"pitfalls":["Feeding low-resolution or skewed images straight in; the symptom is characters merged or split and a CER that halves after simple upscaling and deskewing.","Leaving the default page segmentation mode on multi-column or sparse layouts; the symptom is lines from neighbouring columns interleaved into nonsense sentences.","Flattening a table to text before chunking for RAG; the symptom is retrieved chunks containing numbers with no column header, so the LLM attaches the wrong label to the right value.","Ignoring confidence and treating OCR as truth; the symptom is \"1O2.5O\" style errors reaching a database where a quick confidence gate would have flagged them.","Treating an engine's confidence as a calibrated probability or comparing it across engines; the symptom is a \"95-confidence\" field that is wrong far more often than 5% of the time, found only when you plot error rate against confidence on a labelled sample and set the threshold from that curve.","Forgetting that OCR text is untrusted input; the symptom is an agent that follows instructions printed on a scanned document, a prompt-injection path via images.","Assuming the PDF has no text layer and OCR-ing born-digital pages; the symptom is avoidable errors and cost on text that was already perfect, when a text-layer check would have skipped OCR."],"when":"Use dedicated OCR whenever you need text with positions and confidence from scans, photos or image\nPDFs at volume: search indexing, redaction, extraction into systems of record, and as grounding text\nfor an LLM so it reads rather than guesses. Check first whether the document already has a text layer.\nEscalate to cloud document OCR when tables and forms dominate, and to a VLM when the layout is\nirregular and the output feeds a human rather than a ledger. Measure CER and WER on a labelled sample\nof your own scans, plus the fraction of fields below your confidence threshold, since that is your\nhuman-review workload. In interviews, be ready to walk the pipeline, explain what CTC solves, and say\nhow you would detect that OCR, not the LLM, is the failing stage.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Builds the preprocess-detect-recognise path, chooses segmentation modes per document type, and surfaces confidence and boxes rather than a flat string to downstream code.","architect":"Decides the escalation ladder from cheap engine to cloud OCR to VLM, with data-residency and per-page cost as the constraints, and where human review sits in the flow.","fde":"Profiles the customer's real scan quality, finds the DPI and preprocessing that hit the accuracy plateau, and sets the confidence threshold against their tolerance for review work."},"check":{"q":"An extraction pipeline on scanned purchase orders has acceptable accuracy on item descriptions but\nfrequent errors in part numbers and totals. The team proposes replacing the OCR engine with a VLM. What\nwould you investigate first and why might the swap make things worse?","a":"Part numbers and totals are codes and digits, where two specific OCR behaviours bite: dictionary\nor language-model correction rewrites unfamiliar codes into dictionary-like strings, and low resolution\nmakes 0/O and 1/l indistinguishable. First check the per-field confidence on those regions, disable\ndictionary correction for code fields, upscale and re-OCR the low-confidence crops, and confirm the\ncorrect page segmentation mode so table cells are not merged. A VLM swap can make it worse because a VLM\nunder the same visual ambiguity emits a confident plausible digit with no confidence score, so errors\nstop being detectable. Use the VLM, if at all, only as an escalation for low-confidence regions, and add\narithmetic validation (line items sum to total) so residual errors are caught."},"quiz":[{"q":"What problem does CTC solve in a text-line recogniser?","options":["It detects where text lines are located on the page","It aligns a per-slice sequence of predictions to a shorter character string without labelled character boundaries","It corrects recognised words against a dictionary","It deskews the input image before recognition"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The network emits one output per horizontal slice of the image; CTC collapses repeats and blanks so a variable-width image maps to the right character sequence without knowing where each glyph starts."},{"q":"Columns from a two-column scanned report come out interleaved line by line. Which stage is the most likely culprit?","options":["The character recogniser confusing similar glyphs","Insufficient scan resolution","Layout analysis using a page segmentation mode that assumes a single block of text","The language-model post-processor"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Interleaving is a reading-order error, which is decided by layout analysis before any character is recognised. Resolution and glyph confusion produce wrong characters, not scrambled line order."},{"q":"Why is per-word confidence the main reason to prefer a dedicated OCR engine over a VLM for amounts feeding a ledger?","options":["VLMs cannot read digits","Confidence scores make the OCR engine faster","Confidence lets uncertain values be routed to re-OCR or human review instead of silently accepted","VLMs are not allowed to process financial documents"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A VLM resolves ambiguity by producing a plausible value with no signal that it guessed. A confidence score turns the same ambiguity into a flag, which is what a review workflow needs."}]},{"key":"multimodal/speech-to-text","sources":["Radford et al., 2022 — Robust Speech Recognition via Large-Scale Weak Supervision (Whisper)","Graves et al., 2006 — Connectionist Temporal Classification","Bain et al., 2023 — WhisperX: Time-Accurate Speech Transcription of Long-Form Audio","Official docs: faster-whisper"],"why":"Voice is the input modality for call centres, meetings, dictation, voice agents and accessibility, and\nall of it is useless to text systems until transcribed. Classic ASR pipelines (acoustic model plus\npronunciation lexicon plus language model, as in Kaldi-era systems) delivered good accuracy on the\nconditions they were tuned for and fell apart on a new microphone, accent or vocabulary. They needed\nphonetically aligned data and expert tuning per language, which few teams had.\n\nEnd-to-end neural models (CTC and RNN-transducer models) removed the lexicon but were trained on\ncurated, human-transcribed corpora of a few thousand hours, so they were excellent on their benchmark\nand brittle elsewhere: a model that scores superbly on read audiobook speech can double its error rate\non a noisy phone call. The naive fix, \"fine-tune on your domain\", only works if you have transcribed\naudio, which is exactly what you were trying to produce.\n\nWhisper's bet was scale with weak supervision: collect hundreds of thousands of hours of audio paired\nwith whatever transcripts existed on the web, filter out machine-generated ones, and train one model to\ndo transcription, translation to English and language identification. The pay-off was zero-shot\nrobustness: strong results on datasets it never trained on, which is what a product shipping to unknown\nusers actually needs.","how":"### Input: audio becomes a spectrogram\nAudio is resampled to 16 kHz mono and cut into 30-second windows (padded with silence if shorter). Each\nwindow is converted to a log-Mel spectrogram: a short-time Fourier transform with 25 ms frames every\n10 ms, mapped onto 80 Mel-scaled frequency bands (128 in large-v3), log-compressed and normalised. The\nMel scale mimics human frequency sensitivity, and the log makes loud and quiet speech comparable. The\nresult is a 2-D array of 3,000 frames × 80 features, an \"image\" of the sound that a transformer can read.\n\n### Encoder-decoder\nTwo convolutional layers (the second with stride 2) shrink the time axis to 1,500 positions and then a\ntransformer encoder produces a contextual representation of the whole window. Following the shapes makes\nseveral later behaviours obvious:\n\n```\n30 s × 16,000 samples/s           = 480,000 samples\nhop of 10 ms (160 samples)        → 3,000 spectrogram frames × 80 (or 128) Mel bands\nconv stack, stride 2              → 1,500 encoder positions, each covering 20 ms\n```\n\nEach encoder position spans 20 ms, which is exactly the resolution of the timestamp tokens, and the window\nlength is fixed by the encoder's positional embeddings, which is why audio cannot simply be fed longer. The decoder is a standard autoregressive text\ntransformer that cross-attends to the encoder output and emits text tokens from a byte-pair vocabulary.\nThis is the same architecture as translation: audio in one \"language\", text out in another, and it is\nwhy Whisper can translate to English as a decoding task rather than a separate model.\n\n### The multitask token format\nWhisper controls behaviour through special tokens in the decoder prompt rather than separate heads: a\nstart token, then a language token (predicted if not given, which is how language identification\nworks), then a task token (transcribe or translate), then optionally a no-timestamps token. During\ntranscription the decoder can interleave timestamp tokens with text, quantised to 20 ms, which is what\ngives segment start and end times. A dedicated no-speech token lets the model declare that the window\nis silence or music. Everything the model can do is one sequence-to-sequence problem, which is why one\ncheckpoint serves many languages and tasks.\n\n### Long-form audio and decoding heuristics\nBecause the encoder sees exactly 30 seconds, longer audio is processed as a sliding sequence of windows:\ntranscribe a window, read the last predicted timestamp, advance the window to that point, and carry the\nprevious text as decoder context for consistency (the decoder's text context is small, so this carried\nprompt, like an `initial_prompt`, is truncated to roughly the last couple of hundred tokens). This is also\nwhere failures concentrate. The decoder is\na language model, so on silence, music or garbled audio it can hallucinate fluent text or loop on a\nphrase. The reference implementation guards against this with heuristics: if the output's compression\nratio is too high (repetition), or the average log-probability too low, decoding is retried at higher\ntemperature; if the no-speech probability is high (and, in the reference implementation, the average\nlog-probability is also low) the window is treated as silence and skipped. Voice activity detection\n(VAD) before transcription removes most of the silence that triggers hallucinations.\n\n### Model sizes and runtimes\nWhisper ships from tiny (tens of millions of parameters) to large (about 1.5 billion). Smaller models\nare several times faster and noticeably less accurate, especially on accents and non-English speech.\nOptimised runtimes (faster-whisper on CTranslate2, whisper.cpp for CPUs and Apple silicon) deliver the\nsame weights with lower memory and higher throughput; WhisperX adds forced alignment with a phoneme\nmodel for accurate word timestamps and plugs in speaker diarization, which Whisper itself does not do.\n\n### Streaming is not native\nA 30-second encoder window means Whisper is a batch model. \"Streaming\" with it means chunking audio into\nshort pieces with overlap and re-decoding, which adds latency and can flicker. Models built for\nstreaming (RNN-transducer or chunked-attention encoders, as in several managed ASR APIs) emit words as\nthey arrive with sub-second delay. If you are building a live voice agent, that difference drives the\narchitecture more than the error rate does.\n\n### Measuring it\nWord error rate is (substitutions + deletions + insertions) / reference words, computed after text\nnormalisation (casing, punctuation, number formats), otherwise \"twenty five\" versus \"25\" counts as two\nerrors. Track WER on your own audio by domain, and separately track entity accuracy (names, product\ncodes), which WER underweights but users notice most.","example":"Transcribe a call recording with faster-whisper, using VAD to suppress silence hallucinations and word\ntimestamps for alignment, then write out segments. The `initial_prompt` seeds the decoder with domain\nvocabulary so product names are spelled consistently.\n\n```python\nfrom faster_whisper import WhisperModel\n\nmodel = WhisperModel(\"large-v3\", device=\"cuda\", compute_type=\"float16\")  # or \"int8\" on CPU\n\nsegments, info = model.transcribe(\n    \"support_call.wav\",\n    beam_size=5,\n    vad_filter=True,                       # drop silence before decoding: fewer hallucinations\n    vad_parameters={\"min_silence_duration_ms\": 500},\n    word_timestamps=True,\n    initial_prompt=\"Acme Cloud, Kubernetes, Terraform, ticket ID, SLA.\",  # vocabulary hint\n    condition_on_previous_text=False,      # trade context for resistance to repetition loops\n)\nprint(f\"language={info.language} p={info.language_probability:.2f} duration={info.duration:.0f}s\")\n\ntranscript = []\nfor seg in segments:                       # a generator: decoding happens as you iterate\n    # Same signals the reference decoder uses for fallback: low avg log-prob or repetitive output.\n    flag = \" [low-conf]\" if seg.avg_logprob < -1.0 or seg.compression_ratio > 2.4 else \"\"\n    shaky = [w.word.strip() for w in (seg.words or []) if w.probability < 0.5]  # review candidates\n    transcript.append(f\"[{seg.start:7.2f} -> {seg.end:7.2f}]{flag} {seg.text.strip()}\"\n                      + (f\"  (check: {', '.join(shaky)})\" if shaky else \"\"))\n\nopen(\"support_call.txt\", \"w\").write(\"\\n\".join(transcript))\nprint(len(transcript), \"segments\")\n# Throughput: real-time factor = wall time / info.duration; at RTF 0.05 one GPU stream clears an hour of\n# audio in ~3 minutes (illustrative), and that, times concurrency, is what you size hardware against.\n# WER check (on a labelled sample): normalise both texts (case, punctuation, numerals) before scoring,\n# e.g. with the jiwer package; report per-domain WER and separately the error rate on entity names.\n```","tradeoffs":["Weak supervision at scale buys zero-shot robustness, but a model fine-tuned on a few hundred hours of your domain's audio will still beat it on your jargon when you can afford the transcription work.","Larger checkpoints cut error rate, especially off-English, at several times the compute; for clean English narration a small model is often indistinguishable and far cheaper.","Batch encoder-decoder design gives high accuracy with context, while native streaming models give sub-second partial results; a live agent needs the latter even at a slightly higher WER.","Conditioning on previous text improves consistency across windows but propagates repetition loops; turn it off when inputs contain long silences or music."],"pitfalls":["Transcribing without VAD; the symptom is fluent sentences appearing in silent stretches, often generic phrases that never occurred in the audio.","Letting language detection run on the first 30 seconds only; the symptom is a bilingual call transcribed in the wrong language, or a translation when you wanted a transcription.","Trusting Whisper's own segment timestamps for word-level alignment; the symptom is subtitles or redactions offset by a second or more, fixed by forced alignment (WhisperX-style).","Scoring WER without normalisation; the symptom is a model that reads better to humans but \"scores worse\" because it wrote numerals and punctuation differently from the reference.","Feeding 8 kHz telephone audio without considering bandwidth; the symptom is a higher error rate on calls than on meetings with no code change, because half the spectrum the model expects is missing.","Treating a transcript as speaker-attributed when it is not; the symptom is a summary that credits the customer with the agent's promise, because Whisper emits one undifferentiated stream and diarization (who spoke when) is a separate model whose errors cluster at fast turn-taking and crosstalk.","Forgetting the transcript contains PII; the symptom is a compliance finding over stored call text that nobody classified, since the audio was treated as the sensitive asset and the text was not."],"when":"Use Whisper-class models for offline or near-real-time transcription where accuracy across accents,\nnoise and languages matters more than sub-second latency: call analytics, meeting notes, media\nindexing, voice memos, and as the first stage before an LLM summarises or extracts. Choose a\nstreaming-native model or managed streaming API for live voice agents and captions. Measure WER on\nyour own normalised audio per domain, entity accuracy, hallucination rate on silence, and real-time\nfactor (processing time divided by audio duration) at your target concurrency. In interviews, be able\nto explain the spectrogram-encoder-decoder flow, why 30-second windows cause both the long-form\nheuristics and the streaming problem, and what weak supervision bought.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Wires VAD, chunking and decoding options, aligns timestamps for downstream features, and monitors WER and hallucination rate on sampled production audio.","architect":"Picks batch versus streaming ASR per product surface, decides self-hosted versus managed on cost, latency and data residency, and sets the latency budget for voice flows.","fde":"Collects a labelled sample of the customer's real calls, measures WER by accent and line quality, and tunes vocabulary prompts and thresholds before promising accuracy figures."},"check":{"q":"A team transcribes hour-long recorded support calls with Whisper and reports two problems:\nsentences that were never spoken appear during hold music, and a product name is spelled three\ndifferent ways. How would you address each, and what metric would you add?","a":"The phantom sentences are decoder hallucinations: the decoder is a language model and, given\nmusic or silence, continues generating plausible text. Add voice activity detection so non-speech is\nnever decoded, keep the compression-ratio and log-probability fallbacks on, consider disabling\nconditioning on previous text so a loop cannot propagate, and drop segments the model marks as\nno-speech. The spelling drift comes from the decoder choosing among acoustically similar spellings with\nno prior; seed the decoder with an initial prompt that contains the product name and other domain\nterms, and apply a post-transcription normalisation map for known entities. Add entity accuracy on a\nlabelled sample as a metric beside normalised WER, because WER barely moves on a single misspelled\nproduct name while users and search break on it."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why does Whisper process audio in fixed 30-second windows?","options":["Audio files longer than 30 seconds cannot be stored as spectrograms","The encoder consumes a fixed-size spectrogram, so longer audio is handled by sliding windows guided by predicted timestamps","Human speech naturally pauses every 30 seconds","The decoder vocabulary only covers 30 seconds of text"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The encoder expects a fixed 3,000-frame input; long-form transcription advances a window using the last timestamp the decoder emitted. This design is also why streaming is not native."},{"q":"What is the most effective first step against hallucinated text during silence or music?","options":["Switch to a larger Whisper checkpoint","Increase the beam size","Run voice activity detection and skip non-speech regions before decoding","Lower the sampling rate to 8 kHz"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Hallucinations occur because the decoder generates language even when the encoder saw no speech; removing non-speech input removes the trigger. Larger models and beams do not change this behaviour."},{"q":"How did Whisper's training approach differ from earlier end-to-end ASR models, and what did it buy?","options":["It used a phonetic lexicon to improve pronunciation modelling","It trained on a very large, weakly labelled multilingual corpus, buying zero-shot robustness across domains","It was trained only on a small curated audiobook dataset for maximum accuracy","It replaced the transformer with a recurrent network for streaming"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Earlier models trained on curated thousands of hours and were brittle off-benchmark; Whisper scaled to hundreds of thousands of weakly supervised hours and generalised to unseen datasets without tuning."}]},{"key":"multimodal/tts","sources":["Shen et al., 2018 — Natural TTS Synthesis by Conditioning WaveNet on Mel Spectrogram Predictions (Tacotron 2)","Kong et al., 2020 — HiFi-GAN: Generative Adversarial Networks for Efficient and High Fidelity Speech Synthesis","Kim et al., 2021 — Conditional Variational Autoencoder with Adversarial Learning for End-to-End Text-to-Speech (VITS)","Wang et al., 2023 — Neural Codec Language Models are Zero-Shot Text to Speech Synthesizers (VALL-E)"],"why":"A voice agent that can hear but not speak is a chatbot. The first generations of synthesis either\nglued recorded snippets together (concatenative) or simulated the vocal tract with rules (formant\nsynthesis). Concatenation sounds human on the exact phrases that were recorded and seams badly between\nthem; rules are intelligible but unmistakably robotic. Neither can voice an LLM's unbounded, freshly\ngenerated sentences with natural rhythm and emphasis.\n\nThe problem has three hard parts that a naive \"look up each word's sound\" approach ignores. First,\nthe text is ambiguous before it is pronounced: \"read\" has two sounds, \"1/2\" might be a date or a\nfraction, \"St.\" might be street or saint. Second, prosody (timing, pitch, loudness) carries meaning,\nand it depends on the whole sentence, not the word. Third, producing raw audio is expensive: 24,000\nsamples per second, each depending on the ones before. Early neural vocoders that generated sample by\nsample sounded excellent and were far too slow for anything interactive.\n\nNeural TTS exists because learned models solve the first two problems from data and the third by\nsplitting the job into a cheap sequence model over a compressed representation plus a fast decoder.\nFor an AI engineer the stakes are concrete: time-to-first-audio and pronunciation errors are what make a\nvoice agent feel broken, regardless of how good the LLM behind it is.","how":"### Stage 1: text normalisation and front end\nThe raw text is rewritten into what should be *said*: numbers expanded (\"$1,250.50\" to \"one thousand two\nhundred fifty dollars and fifty cents\", \"2024\" as a year or a quantity depending on context),\nabbreviations resolved, symbols and markdown removed, sentence boundaries found. A grapheme-to-phoneme\nstep then converts words to phonemes using a lexicon plus a learned model for unseen words. This stage\nis rule-heavy and language-specific, and it is where most \"the voice said something wrong\" bugs live;\nneural models downstream faithfully pronounce whatever the front end hands them.\n\n### Stage 2: acoustic model\nThe phoneme (or character) sequence is mapped to an intermediate acoustic representation, classically\na mel-spectrogram: a time-frequency picture at roughly 80 bands per frame and on the order of a hundred\nframes per second of speech (a 10–12.5 ms hop). At 24 kHz that is a couple of hundred times fewer\n*time steps* than the waveform; each step is a vector of ~80 values, so the raw value count is only a few\ntimes smaller. The saving that matters is sequence length, because that is what an attention or\nautoregressive model pays for, and the smooth spectral envelope is far easier to predict than\nindividual samples with their phase. Two designs matter. Autoregressive models (Tacotron 2) generate frames one at a time\nwith attention over the input text; natural, but slow and prone to skipping or repeating words when\nattention wanders. Non-autoregressive models (FastSpeech 2) first predict each phoneme's *duration*,\nexpand the sequence accordingly, predict pitch and energy per phoneme, and then generate all frames in\nparallel. They exist because explicit duration makes synthesis fast, controllable and immune to the\nskipping failure, at the cost of a slightly flatter delivery unless the prosody predictors are good.\n\n### Stage 3: vocoder\nThe spectrogram is an abstraction without phase; a vocoder reconstructs the waveform. WaveNet did it\nautoregressively per sample with superb quality and impractical latency. GAN vocoders (HiFi-GAN) upsample\nthe spectrogram with convolutions in one pass, trained with a discriminator that judges realism; they run\nfar faster than real time on a GPU and are the default in production pipelines.\n\n### End-to-end and codec-language-model designs\nVITS trains acoustic model and vocoder jointly as one variational model with normalising flows, removing\nthe spectrogram seam and the quality loss from mismatched stages. The more recent family (VALL-E and its\ndescendants, and most \"voice cloning\" products) treats TTS as language modelling: a neural audio codec\n(EnCodec-style) compresses audio into discrete tokens at a low frame rate, and a decoder-only transformer\npredicts audio tokens conditioned on text tokens plus a few seconds of reference audio. Because the\nreference is just a prompt, the model clones an unseen voice zero-shot, with the same hallucination and\nrepetition risks as any autoregressive LM. The codec decoder then turns tokens back into waveform.\n\nThe token arithmetic explains the architectures. A codec such as EnCodec at 24 kHz emits 75 frames per\nsecond, and residual vector quantisation stacks several codebooks per frame, each refining the residual\nerror of the previous one (8 codebooks of 1,024 entries give 75 × 8 × 10 bits = 6 kbps). Ten seconds of\nspeech is then 750 frames × 8 = 6,000 tokens, too many to generate one by one at conversational speed.\nVALL-E therefore predicts only the first, coarsest codebook autoregressively (it carries most of the\ncontent and prosody) and fills in the other seven non-autoregressively in parallel; later models use\ninterleaving or \"delay\" patterns across codebooks for the same reason.\n\n### Streaming and latency\nA conversational agent cannot wait for the whole reply to be synthesised. The pipeline is streamed at\ntwo levels: the LLM's text is split at sentence or clause boundaries as it arrives, and each piece is\nsynthesised and played while the next is produced; within a piece, chunked vocoding emits audio in\nsmall frames. The metrics are time-to-first-audio (TTFA) and real-time factor (RTF = synthesis time /\naudio duration; below 1 means faster than real time, and the margin is your concurrency headroom).\nBarge-in, stopping playback and generation when the user starts talking, is a product requirement that\nthe architecture must support, which means cancellable synthesis.\n\nA worked budget shows why sentence streaming is non-negotiable (illustrative numbers): end-of-speech\ndetection 300 ms, final ASR 200 ms, LLM first token 400 ms, first clause of ~12 tokens at 50 tokens/s\n240 ms, TTS first chunk 150 ms, network and jitter buffer 100 ms gives roughly 1.4 s to first audio.\nIf TTS instead waits for a 150-token reply, the LLM alone adds about 3 s before synthesis starts. The\nlever with the most leverage is usually how early the splitter releases the first chunk (a clause\nboundary rather than a full sentence), not the vocoder.\n\n### Quality and control\nNaturalness is measured by mean opinion score (MOS, listeners rate 1 to 5), intelligibility by running\nASR on the output and computing WER against the input text, and cloning fidelity by cosine similarity\nin a speaker-embedding space. Control comes from SSML-style markup (breaks, emphasis, say-as for\ndigits and dates, explicit phonemes) or from style prompts in newer models.","example":"The engineering around TTS matters as much as the model: normalise text an LLM produced (markdown,\ncurrency, acronyms), chunk at sentence boundaries, and stream chunks through any synthesiser so\ntime-to-first-audio is bounded by the first sentence, not the whole reply. `synthesize` is any function\nreturning PCM bytes for a string; the timing code is what you would ship as metrics.\n\n```python\nimport re, time\nfrom num2words import num2words\n\ndef normalize(text: str) -> str:\n    text = re.sub(r\"[*_`#>]\", \"\", text)                       # strip markdown an LLM may emit\n    text = re.sub(r\"\\$(\\d[\\d,]*)(?:\\.(\\d{2}))?\",                # \"$1,250.50\" -> spoken dollars/cents\n                  lambda m: num2words(int(m.group(1).replace(\",\", \"\"))) + \" dollars\"\n                  + (f\" and {num2words(int(m.group(2)))} cents\" if m.group(2) else \"\"), text)\n    text = re.sub(r\"\\b(\\d+)%\", lambda m: num2words(int(m.group(1))) + \" percent\", text)\n    text = text.replace(\"SLA\", \"S L A\").replace(\"K8s\", \"kubernetes\")   # per-domain lexicon\n    return text\n\ndef sentences(text: str):\n    return [s for s in re.split(r\"(?<=[.!?])\\s+\", text.strip()) if s]\n\ndef stream_speech(reply: str, synthesize, sample_rate=24_000, bytes_per_sample=2):\n    t0, first, audio_s = time.perf_counter(), None, 0.0\n    for s in sentences(reply):\n        pcm = synthesize(normalize(s))                         # any TTS backend: bytes of 16-bit PCM\n        if first is None:\n            first = time.perf_counter() - t0                   # time-to-first-audio\n        audio_s += len(pcm) / (sample_rate * bytes_per_sample)\n        yield pcm                                              # play/send while the next sentence renders\n    rtf = (time.perf_counter() - t0) / max(audio_s, 1e-6)\n    print(f\"TTFA={first:.2f}s  audio={audio_s:.1f}s  RTF={rtf:.2f}\")\n\nreply = \"Your **SLA** credit is $1,250.50. That is 12% of the invoice. K8s nodes are back.\"\nfor chunk in stream_speech(reply, synthesize=lambda s: b\"\\x00\" * 48_000):   # stub: 1 s of silence\n    pass   # in production: write chunk to the WebSocket / audio device\n```","tradeoffs":["Non-autoregressive acoustic models give fast, stable, controllable synthesis, while autoregressive and codec-LM models give richer prosody and zero-shot cloning at higher latency and hallucination risk.","Sentence-level streaming minimises time-to-first-audio but loses cross-sentence prosody; it flips when the content is narration rather than dialogue, where whole-paragraph synthesis sounds better.","Managed TTS APIs deliver quality and voices without GPUs, but per-character pricing, data residency and vendor voice licensing push high-volume or regulated deployments toward self-hosting.","Voice cloning from seconds of audio is a product feature and a liability; consent, verification and watermarking cost engineering effort that a fixed stock voice does not."],"pitfalls":["Sending raw LLM output to the synthesiser; the symptom is the voice reading \"asterisk asterisk\" or spelling out URLs and JSON keys that should never have reached speech.","Skipping number and date normalisation; the symptom is \"two thousand twenty-four\" for a year, phone numbers read as large integers, and \"1/2\" spoken as a date.","Synthesising the whole reply before playback; the symptom is a multi-second pause before every answer that users read as the agent being broken, even though total synthesis time is fine.","Ignoring sample-rate and codec conversion; the symptom is chipmunk or slowed audio on telephony paths that expect 8 kHz μ-law when the model emitted 24 kHz PCM.","No cancellation path for barge-in; the symptom is the agent talking over the user for several seconds after being interrupted.","Evaluating only by listening to a few demos; the symptom is mispronounced customer names and product terms in production that an ASR round-trip WER test on your own vocabulary would have caught."],"when":"Reach for neural TTS whenever text must become speech for dynamic content: voice agents, IVR replacing\nrecorded menus, accessibility read-aloud, generated narration and alerts. Use recorded audio when the\nset of phrases is small and fixed and legal sign-off on exact wording matters. Choose a fast\nnon-autoregressive or streaming-capable model for interactive use and a higher-quality, slower model for\noffline narration. Measure time-to-first-audio, RTF at target concurrency, ASR round-trip WER on your\nown text, and mispronunciation rate on your entity list. Interviews tend to probe the three-stage\npipeline, why vocoders exist, and how you would hit a conversational latency budget.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Owns text normalisation and the lexicon, streams at sentence boundaries, implements cancellation for barge-in, and tracks TTFA and RTF as service metrics.","architect":"Sets the end-to-end voice latency budget across ASR, LLM and TTS, chooses managed versus self-hosted synthesis, and defines the consent and watermarking policy for cloned voices.","fde":"Builds the customer's pronunciation lexicon from their product and people names, validates the voice on their telephony path, and demonstrates the latency budget on real calls."},"check":{"q":"A voice agent's users complain it \"takes forever to answer\" even though logs show LLM first\ntoken at 400 ms and TTS synthesis running at RTF 0.3. What is the likely cause and how would you\nrestructure the pipeline?","a":"RTF 0.3 only says synthesis is faster than playback once it starts; the delay is sequencing. The\npipeline most likely waits for the complete LLM reply, then synthesises the whole text, then plays,\nso perceived latency is LLM generation time plus full synthesis time. Restructure so the LLM streams\ntokens, a splitter emits the first sentence as soon as its boundary appears, TTS synthesises that\nsentence while the LLM continues, and audio plays while the next sentence renders. Time-to-first-audio\nthen approximates first-sentence LLM latency plus one short synthesis. Add cancellation so barge-in\nstops both generation and playback, normalise each chunk before synthesis, and instrument TTFA as the\nmetric that matches what users feel."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why do most TTS pipelines predict a mel-spectrogram first and then run a vocoder, instead of generating waveform samples directly from text?","options":["Waveforms cannot be produced by neural networks","The spectrogram is a compact representation that a sequence model can predict cheaply, and a fast vocoder reconstructs the waveform","Vocoders perform the text normalisation step","Spectrograms contain phase information that text lacks"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Raw audio is tens of thousands of samples per second; predicting a low-rate spectrogram then upsampling it with a one-pass vocoder keeps synthesis fast. End-to-end models exist but share this cost logic."},{"q":"Which design choice most directly prevents the \"skipped or repeated word\" failure of attention-based autoregressive TTS?","options":["A larger vocoder","Training on more speakers","Explicit per-phoneme duration prediction in a non-autoregressive acoustic model","Higher output sample rate"],"answer":2,"explanation":"FastSpeech-style models decide how many frames each phoneme gets before generating, so every phoneme is rendered exactly once; attention drift in Tacotron-style models is what causes skips and repeats."},{"q":"For a conversational agent, which metric best captures what users perceive as responsiveness?","options":["Real-time factor of the vocoder","Mean opinion score of the voice","Time-to-first-audio measured from the end of the user's utterance","Total synthesis time for the full reply"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Users judge the gap before the agent starts speaking. RTF and MOS matter for throughput and quality, but a low RTF with whole-reply synthesis still produces a long silent gap."}]},{"key":"multimodal/document-understanding","sources":["Xu et al., 2020 — LayoutLM: Pre-training of Text and Layout for Document Image Understanding","Huang et al., 2022 — LayoutLMv3: Pre-training for Document AI with Unified Text and Image Masking","Kim et al., 2022 — OCR-free Document Understanding Transformer (Donut)","Mathew et al., 2021 — DocVQA: A Dataset for VQA on Document Images"],"why":"A purchase order is not a paragraph. \"Total\" means the number in the box to the right of the word, on\nthe last page, after the line items, not the first number that follows the word \"total\" in a text\ndump. A contract's clause numbering, a form's checkboxes, a bank statement's columns: in each case the\ninformation is encoded in *where* things are as much as in what they say. Flatten the page to a string\nand that encoding is gone.\n\nThe first generation of document automation accepted this and fought it with templates: for each\nvendor's invoice layout, hand-written coordinates or regexes that located each field. It works until\nthe vendor changes its layout, a new vendor appears, or a scan is slightly skewed, which in practice\nmeans it is always breaking somewhere, and the maintenance cost scales with the number of templates. The\n\"zonal OCR\" business existed because of this treadmill.\n\nThe second naive alternative arrived with LLMs: OCR the page and ask the model to extract fields from\nthe text. Better, but the LLM now reasons over a reading order that OCR guessed, over tables that have\nbecome interleaved numbers, with no idea which value sat in which column. It also returns answers with\ntotal confidence and no mechanism to say a field was absent. Document understanding as a discipline\nexists to put layout back into the model's input and to put verification around the model's output.","how":"### Three model families\n1. **OCR plus layout-aware encoder (LayoutLM family).** OCR provides words and bounding boxes. The model\n   embeds each token with its text, its 2-D position and, in later versions, image patch features of\n   the page. Concretely, box coordinates are normalised to a 0–1000 grid regardless of page size, and\n   learned embeddings for x0, y0, x1, y1 (plus width and height in v2) are summed with the token\n   embedding: `e = E_tok(w) + E_x(x0) + E_y(y0) + E_x(x1) + E_y(y1) + E_pos(i)`. Normalising to a grid\n   is a deliberate choice, because it makes \"top-right corner\" mean the same thing on an A4 scan and a\n   phone photo, and summing rather than concatenating lets self-attention compare positions with the\n   same dot products it uses for meaning. Pretraining masks text and image regions\n   so the model learns to associate words with positions and appearance. Fine-tuned heads then do token\n   classification for key information extraction (label each word as invoice_number, total, none),\n   page classification, or extractive question answering. It inherits OCR's errors but also its\n   confidences and boxes, which is why it remains common in regulated extraction.\n2. **OCR-free end-to-end (Donut, Pix2Struct).** A vision encoder reads the page image and a text decoder\n   generates the output directly, trained to emit a serialised JSON of the fields. No OCR stage means no\n   reading-order errors and one model to run, but also no per-word confidence and a fixed resolution\n   that limits fine print.\n3. **General vision-language models with structured prompts.** A VLM receives the page image (and often\n   the OCR text as extra context) and a schema, and returns JSON. Zero-shot across templates, strong on\n   irregular layouts, highest per-page cost and the strongest tendency to invent a value when the field\n   is missing. Document parsers (cloud Document AI services, open-source layout toolkits) sit beside\n   these as pre-processors that emit text, blocks, tables and reading order the VLM can consume.\n\n### The pipeline around the model\nThe model is one stage. A production path looks like: ingest and classify the file type; render PDF\npages to images at a resolution that preserves small text (around 150–200 DPI is a common starting\npoint, tuned per corpus); detect whether a text layer exists so born-digital pages skip OCR; run OCR\nwhere needed; run layout analysis to find blocks (title, paragraph, table, figure, header, footer) and\nreading order; recover table structure (rows, columns, spanning cells) separately, because tables fail\ndifferently from prose; split multi-document packets by page classification; extract against an explicit\nschema; validate; route. Each stage exists because a failure there has a distinct signature, and\nisolating stages is what makes the failure diagnosable.\n\n### Schema-driven extraction and validation\nExtraction is only useful when the output is typed and checkable. Define the schema with explicit\nnullability so the model can say \"absent\" instead of guessing, request structured output, and validate\nwith code: types and formats (dates, currency), cross-field arithmetic (line item quantities × unit\nprices summed against the subtotal, subtotal plus tax against the total), referential checks against\nsystems of record (a vendor ID that exists), and consistency across pages. Each failed check becomes a\nreason to route the document to review. This is the layer that converts a probabilistic model into a\nprocess with a known error budget, and the straight-through-processing rate (documents needing no human\ntouch) is the business metric that falls out of it.\n\n### Getting a confidence signal back\nFamilies 2 and 3 return values without confidence, and review routing needs one. Practical substitutes,\nin rising cost: ask for provenance (the page number and the verbatim source text or box for each field)\nand check that the quoted text really exists in the OCR or text layer, which catches most invented\nvalues cheaply; read token log-probabilities for the value tokens where the serving stack exposes them;\nrun two extractions (two prompts, two models, or VLM versus OCR-plus-layout) and treat disagreement as\nlow confidence. None of these is calibrated out of the box, so plot error rate against the signal on a\nlabelled sample before choosing a routing threshold.\n\n### Evaluation\nField-level exact match or F1 for extraction, with per-field breakdown because \"total\" and \"notes\" do\nnot matter equally. For question answering on document images the standard metric is ANLS, a\nnormalised edit-distance score that tolerates minor OCR-style spelling differences. For tables, TEDS\nmeasures structural similarity as a tree edit distance over cell structure. Build the evaluation set\nfrom real customer documents with reviewer labels, stratified by template and scan quality, because\npublic benchmarks will not contain your vendors' layouts.\n\n### Retrieval over documents\nFor \"find the clause about termination in these 3,000 PDFs\", the same choices recur at index time: chunk\nOCR text with layout-aware boundaries so a table and its header stay together, or embed page images\ndirectly with a document-screenshot embedder and let retrieval happen over pixels. The deep treatment\nof chunking, parsing and ingestion lives in the retrieval and data-architecture modules; this lesson is\nabout getting trustworthy structure out of a page.","example":"A minimal extraction path for one invoice page: detect whether the PDF already has text (skip OCR if\nso), render the page, ask a VLM served behind an OpenAI-compatible API for a schema-shaped JSON with\nexplicit nulls, then validate with pydantic including the arithmetic check that catches most\nhallucinated amounts. Review routing is the output, not just the fields.\n\n```python\nimport base64, json, fitz                     # PyMuPDF\nfrom pydantic import BaseModel, ValidationError\nfrom openai import OpenAI\n\nclass Line(BaseModel):\n    description: str; quantity: float; unit_price: float; amount: float\nclass Invoice(BaseModel):\n    invoice_number: str | None; invoice_date: str | None; currency: str | None\n    subtotal: float | None; tax: float | None; total: float | None; lines: list[Line]\n\nSCHEMA = json.dumps(Invoice.model_json_schema())\nclient = OpenAI(base_url=\"http://localhost:8000/v1\", api_key=\"unused\")\n\ndef extract(pdf_path, page_no=0):\n    page = fitz.open(pdf_path)[page_no]\n    has_text_layer = len(page.get_text().strip()) > 50         # born-digital: text is already exact\n    png = page.get_pixmap(dpi=200).tobytes(\"png\")               # resolution preserves small print\n    prompt = (\"Extract this invoice page into JSON matching the schema. Use null for any field not \"\n              \"visibly present; never guess. Dates as YYYY-MM-DD.\\nSchema: \" + SCHEMA)\n    content = [{\"type\": \"text\", \"text\": prompt},\n               {\"type\": \"image_url\", \"image_url\": {\"url\": \"data:image/png;base64,\"\n                                                   + base64.b64encode(png).decode()}}]\n    if has_text_layer:                                          # give exact text as grounding\n        content.insert(1, {\"type\": \"text\", \"text\": \"Page text:\\n\" + page.get_text()})\n    r = client.chat.completions.create(model=\"<served-vlm>\", temperature=0,\n                                       messages=[{\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": content}])\n    try:\n        inv = Invoice.model_validate_json(r.choices[0].message.content)\n    except ValidationError as e:\n        return None, [\"schema: \" + str(e)[:120]]\n    issues = []\n    if inv.lines and inv.subtotal is not None and abs(sum(l.amount for l in inv.lines) - inv.subtotal) > 0.01:\n        issues.append(\"lines do not sum to subtotal\")\n    if None not in (inv.subtotal, inv.tax, inv.total) and abs(inv.subtotal + inv.tax - inv.total) > 0.01:\n        issues.append(\"subtotal + tax != total\")\n    text = page.get_text().replace(\",\", \"\") if has_text_layer else \"\"\n    if text and inv.total is not None and f\"{inv.total:.2f}\" not in text:\n        issues.append(\"total not found verbatim in text layer\")    # cheap provenance check\n    return inv, issues                                          # issues non-empty -> human review queue\n\ninv, issues = extract(\"po_2024_0417.pdf\")\nprint(inv.total if inv else None, issues)\n```","tradeoffs":["Layout-aware encoders on OCR output keep confidences and boxes for audit, but inherit OCR errors and need labelled fine-tuning per task; VLMs skip labelling and lose the confidence signal.","OCR-free models remove reading-order failures and run as one stage, yet their fixed input resolution loses fine print, which flips the decision on dense statements and engineering drawings.","Schema-driven extraction with validation raises straight-through rates and catches hallucinated amounts, but every rule is maintenance; keep rules to checks the business can state as invariants.","Higher render resolution and tiling improve small-text accuracy at multiplied token cost per page; find the plateau on your corpus and render at that, not at maximum."],"pitfalls":["Rendering pages at screen resolution; the symptom is correct headers and wrong digits, because small numerals fall below the encoder's effective resolution.","Letting the model fill absent fields; the symptom is invoices with a plausible tax amount that does not appear anywhere on the page, found only when the ledger does not reconcile.","Treating multi-page packets as one document; the symptom is a total extracted from the wrong invoice in a stapled scan, fixed by page classification and splitting before extraction.","Flattening tables before extraction; the symptom is quantities and prices swapped between columns, or line items merged, especially on tables with wrapped descriptions.","Measuring an average accuracy only; the symptom is a release that improved overall F1 while getting worse on the one field, the total, that drives the business outcome.","Accepting VLM-extracted values with no provenance; the symptom is reviewers spending minutes hunting for where a field came from, and invented values that pass every format check because nothing ties them to a location on the page.","Assuming the document is trustworthy input; the symptom is an extraction agent obeying instructions printed in a submitted PDF, the document-borne form of prompt injection."],"when":"Use a document-understanding pipeline when the output must be structured and acted upon: accounts\npayable, claims, KYC onboarding, contract review, forms processing, and any RAG system over scanned\nmaterial where tables carry the answers. For a handful of pages a human reads anyway, a plain VLM call\nis enough; for regulated extraction at volume, the full pipeline with validation and review routing is\nthe product. Decide the model family by whether you need confidences and audit trails (layout-aware on\nOCR) or zero-shot coverage across unknown templates (VLM), and often use both: VLM for breadth,\nvalidation for trust. Measure per-field accuracy, straight-through rate, and reviewer time per\ndocument. In interviews, be ready to explain why layout matters, what validation catches that models\ncannot, and how you would evaluate on a customer's own documents.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Implements rendering, text-layer detection, schema-driven extraction and the validation rules, and emits review reasons rather than raw fields to the queue.","architect":"Chooses the model family and parser per document class, defines the schema contract with downstream systems, and sets the straight-through-rate and cost-per-document targets.","fde":"Assembles a labelled sample from the customer's real documents across templates and scan quality, measures per-field accuracy, and tunes thresholds against their review capacity."},"check":{"q":"A customer's invoice extraction reaches 96% field accuracy on a benchmark but finance rejects it\nbecause wrong totals reach the ledger about twice a day. Design the changes that would make the system\nacceptable without necessarily improving the model.","a":"The gap is between accuracy and *trust*: a 4% error rate with no detection is unacceptable for a\nmoney field, while a 4% error rate that is always caught is fine. Add verification independent of the\nmodel: arithmetic invariants (line items sum to subtotal, subtotal plus tax equals total), format and\nrange checks, duplicate-invoice detection against the ledger, and vendor master-data lookups. Make the\nschema nullable so absence is representable and prohibit guessing in the prompt. Route any failed\ncheck to human review with the reason attached, and report straight-through rate and escaped-error rate\ninstead of average accuracy. Then stratify evaluation by field, with the total weighted as the\nacceptance criterion. Finance accepts a system whose failures are visible and bounded; raising model\naccuracy alone would still leave silent errors."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why does the LayoutLM family add 2-D position embeddings to each OCR token?","options":["To compress the input sequence so long pages fit in the context","Because the meaning of a token on a document depends on where it sits relative to other tokens","To replace the need for a text encoder","To encode the scan's DPI and skew angle"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The number next to \"Total\" and the number inside a line item are distinguished by position, not by text. Position embeddings give the transformer that signal; DPI and skew are preprocessing concerns."},{"q":"What is the main advantage an OCR-free model like Donut gives up compared with an OCR-plus-layout pipeline?","options":["The ability to read printed text at all","Support for languages other than English","Per-word confidence scores and bounding boxes usable for audit and review routing","Handling of multi-page documents"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Generating JSON directly from pixels removes the OCR stage and its reading-order errors, but the confidences and coordinates that the OCR stage produced go with it, which audit workflows rely on."},{"q":"An extraction system returns a tax amount for invoices that show no tax line. Which change addresses the root cause rather than the symptom?","options":["Increase the render resolution","Switch to a larger vision-language model","Make the field nullable in the schema, instruct the model never to guess, and validate subtotal + tax = total","Lower the temperature to zero"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The model is filling a required slot with a plausible value. Representing absence explicitly and checking an invariant turns an invented value into a detectable failure; resolution and size do not."}]}]});
